From: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] frontends/cmn: keep a bridged flow with a local address on the host

The bridge post-routing hook creates a bridged connection for every IP
packet forwarded between two ports of a bridge, whatever its addresses.
One of them can be an address of this very box. On an access point with
the LAN in VLAN 10 and a guest SSID in VLAN 99 of one VLAN-aware bridge,
both on the uplink to the router, a guest that talks to the access
point's own address sends the packet to the router; the access point
bridges it from the VAP to the uplink in VLAN 99, the router routes it
and sends it back on the same cable in VLAN 10, and now it is host
traffic. The same 5-tuple crosses the box twice.

The firmware finds a connection by the 5-tuple alone. Once the bridged
leg is accelerated, the packet that comes back for the host matches the
rule too and is sent out of the uplink again, in VLAN 99, with the L2
header it arrived with: source MAC of the router, destination MAC of the
access point. The router's switch learns its own MAC on the port to the
access point and drops every unicast frame the guest network sends to
the router until the entry ages out, five minutes later; stopping ECM
does not bring it back.

Do not create a connection for a bridged packet when its source or its
destination is a local address.

Signed-off-by: Stanislaw Pal <kuncy7@gmail.com>
--- a/frontends/cmn/ecm_ipv4.c
+++ b/frontends/cmn/ecm_ipv4.c
@@ -1097,6 +1097,29 @@ ecm_ipv4_retry_regen:
 }

 /*
+ * ecm_ipv4_bridged_flow_is_local()
+ *	True when one end of a bridged flow is an address of this host.
+ *
+ * Such a packet is not ours yet: it is on its way to a router that sends it
+ * back to us (our address sits in another VLAN or subnet than the sender),
+ * or it is our own reply on the last leg to a bridged client. The same
+ * 5-tuple therefore crosses this box twice, once bridged and once as host
+ * traffic, and a rule for the bridged leg also catches the other one.
+ */
+static bool ecm_ipv4_bridged_flow_is_local(struct ecm_tracker_ip_header *ip_hdr)
+{
+	__be32 addr;
+
+	ECM_IP_ADDR_TO_NIN4_ADDR(addr, ip_hdr->dest_addr);
+	if (inet_addr_type(&init_net, addr) == RTN_LOCAL) {
+		return true;
+	}
+
+	ECM_IP_ADDR_TO_NIN4_ADDR(addr, ip_hdr->src_addr);
+	return inet_addr_type(&init_net, addr) == RTN_LOCAL;
+}
+
+/*
  * ecm_ipv4_ip_process()
  *	Process IP datagram skb
  */
@@ -1145,6 +1168,15 @@ unsigned int ecm_ipv4_ip_process(struct
 		return NF_ACCEPT;
 	}

+	/*
+	 * A bridged flow to or from one of our own addresses stays on the host.
+	 */
+	if (!is_routed && ecm_ipv4_bridged_flow_is_local(&ip_hdr)) {
+		DEBUG_TRACE("skb %px: bridged flow with a local address, ignoring\n", skb);
+		ecm_stats_v4_inc(ECM_STATS_V4_EXCEPTION_CMN, ECM_STATS_V4_EXCEPTION_BRIDGE_LOCAL_PACKETS_IGNORED);
+		return NF_ACCEPT;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Extract information, if we have conntrack then use that info as far as we can.
 	 */
--- a/frontends/cmn/ecm_ipv6.c
+++ b/frontends/cmn/ecm_ipv6.c
@@ -1084,6 +1084,27 @@ ecm_ipv6_retry_regen:
 }

 /*
+ * ecm_ipv6_bridged_flow_is_local()
+ *	True when one end of a bridged flow is an address of this host.
+ *
+ * See ecm_ipv4_bridged_flow_is_local(): the same 5-tuple crosses this box
+ * twice, once bridged and once as host traffic, and a rule for the bridged
+ * leg also catches the other one.
+ */
+static bool ecm_ipv6_bridged_flow_is_local(struct ecm_tracker_ip_header *ip_hdr)
+{
+	struct in6_addr addr;
+
+	ECM_IP_ADDR_TO_NIN6_ADDR(addr, ip_hdr->dest_addr);
+	if (ipv6_chk_addr(&init_net, &addr, NULL, 0)) {
+		return true;
+	}
+
+	ECM_IP_ADDR_TO_NIN6_ADDR(addr, ip_hdr->src_addr);
+	return ipv6_chk_addr(&init_net, &addr, NULL, 0);
+}
+
+/*
  * ecm_ipv6_ip_process()
  *	Process IP datagram skb
  */
@@ -1133,6 +1154,15 @@ unsigned int ecm_ipv6_ip_process(struct
 		return NF_ACCEPT;
 	}

+	/*
+	 * A bridged flow to or from one of our own addresses stays on the host.
+	 */
+	if (!is_routed && ecm_ipv6_bridged_flow_is_local(&ip_hdr)) {
+		DEBUG_TRACE("skb %px: bridged flow with a local address, ignoring\n", skb);
+		ecm_stats_v6_inc(ECM_STATS_V6_EXCEPTION_CMN, ECM_STATS_V6_EXCEPTION_BRIDGE_LOCAL_PACKETS_IGNORED);
+		return NF_ACCEPT;
+	}
+
 	/*
 	 * Extract information, if we have conntrack then use that info as far as we can.
 	 */
